**Dual-write:** Legacy still serves every read and every write. Each write also adds an `orders_outbox` row in the same legacy transaction. `outbox-relay` then applies those rows to orders-v2 in commit order, while `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so mistakes there are invisible to customers. The phase ends when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- `order-diff` mismatches stay under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full checksum comparison matches.

**Cutover:** Reads move first. `orders.read_from_v2` ramps from 1% to 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, and writes still go to both stores. Reads then soak at 100% for 48 hours. After that, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, so orders-v2 becomes the only store receiving writes. Legacy stays up read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned. The cutover is done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and an order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

**Caveat:** You can roll back only until the write stop, by setting `orders.read_from_v2` back to 0. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` is one-way, because nothing syncs orders-v2 back to legacy. That is why it waits for the soak and needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.
